                T R AC K   “Pressure Drop” — Toots and the Maytals
                       ECHO      the school auditorium, 1975




Our block got mail the way a patient gets news.
    The café received a notice of inspection for an “alleged obstruction”
in the basement, which Mr. Rodríguez had already photographed clear,
dated, and emailed to himself, because he is a poet and therefore a man
who expects to be audited. Denise at the arts space was told, in a very
warm email, that her next grant installment would follow “community
participation” on June thirteenth. Doña Luz got a phone call asking
whether Mikey’s household stipend was “still serving your family,”
from a man who sounded as if he were offering her a refill. Mamá’s
phone buzzed at nine each night with a small green leaf: Calm Block
misses you.
    She held the phone up to the kitchen light each time like a cop
checking a bill.
    “Mamá. Delete it.”
    “No.”
    “Why not?”
    “So I can watch it,” she said, and set the phone face-down on the
counter, next to the radio she had started leaving on all day.
    None of it was a threat. That was the genius. Every envelope was po‐
